    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The circuit pictured below consists of a resistor with
                resistance <m>R</m>, a capacitor with capacitance <m>C</m>, and
                a battery with a constant voltage of <m>V_0</m>.
                Assume there is no initial charge on the capacitor and the
                switch is closed at time 0 so that the current starts flowing
                through the circuit ( <m>I</m> in the picture).
                Let <m>Q(t)</m> be the charge on the capacitor at time
                <m>t \geq 0</m>.
                Physics tells us that <m>{-V_0 + RI + \frac{Q}{C} = 0,}</m>
                where <m>I</m> is actually <m>Q'(t) = \frac{dQ}{dt}</m>.
                Since <m>Q(0)=0</m>, this simplifies to the initial value
                problem:
                <md>
                    \frac{dQ}{dt} = \frac{V_0}{R} - \frac{Q}{RC}, \,\,\,\,\, Q(0) = 0.
                </md>
            </p>

            <image source="images/HW11.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>Unlabeled sample graph.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Identify the dependent variable, the independent variable,
                    and the parameters (constants).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the fixed point(s) of the differential equation and
                    label it/them <m>Q^*</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine if your fixed point(s) are stable or unstable.
                    How do you know?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    This is a linear differential equation that we can solve
                    analytically.
                    Solve this differential equation analytically, either by
                    hand or using any differential equation solver.
                    I'd recommend Mathematica if you've used it before, or
                    wolframalpha.com.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now, only after you have found a general solution for any
                    <m>V_0, R, C</m>, take <m>V_0 = 2.0, R = 100,</m> and
                    <m>C = 0.00001</m>.
                    Adapt the code below to plot the numerical solution to your
                    model <c>y_numerical</c>, as well as the analytical solution
                    to your model found in Part (d), called <c>y_analytic</c>.
                    The first graph will show both your analytical solution and
                    your numerical solution.
                    The second graph will show the difference between them, that
                    is the error of your numerical solution.
                    What do you notice?
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <code>
